Use GitHub Actions OIDC to authenticate to AWS so your workflows can access AWS resources without storing long-lived GitHub secrets [1][2][3][5].

- In your workflow, grant `id-token: write` permission and use `aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v6` with a role ARN and region [4][V].
- In AWS IAM, configure a trust policy for the GitHub OIDC provider that allows `sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity` and matches your repository in the `sub` condition [1][4].
- The trust policy should use the GitHub OIDC provider as the federated principal and include `token.actions.githubusercontent.com:aud` set to `sts.amazonaws.com` [1].
- GitHub Actions will then use the OIDC token to assume the specified role with short-term credentials [4].

Based on [1][4][V]:
```yaml
permissions:
  id-token: write

steps:
  - name: Configure AWS credentials with OIDC
    uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v6
    with:
      role-to-assume: my-role
      aws-region: my-region
```

Based on [1]:
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "Federated": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:oidc-provider/token.actions.githubusercontent.com"
      },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "token.actions.githubusercontent.com:aud": "sts.amazonaws.com"
        },
        "StringLike": {
          "token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub": "repo:your-org/your-repo:*"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

Use the CDK `NodejsFunction` construct, which automatically transpiles and bundles TypeScript or JavaScript code using esbuild, and deploy it as a CDK v2 app with the current pinned package `aws/aws-cdk@v2` [4][V].

Based on [4] and [V]:

```ts
import { NodejsFunction } from 'aws-cdk-lib/aws-lambda-nodejs';

new NodejsFunction(this, 'my-handler', {
  // your function code here
});
```

If you’re creating a regular Lambda function, the CDK `lambda.Code.fromAsset(path)` approach bundles code from an asset path, and the Lambda runtime in the durable-functions CDK example is `lambda.Runtime.NODEJS_22_X` [2][3][V].

Use `aws-cdk-lib/aws-ecs-patterns` and create an `ApplicationLoadBalancedFargateService` with the CDK v2 package pin `aws/aws-cdk@v2` [V][1][2].

Based on [2]:

```ts
import * as ecs from 'aws-cdk-lib/aws-ecs';
import * as ecsPatterns from 'aws-cdk-lib/aws-ecs-patterns';

const cluster = new ecs.Cluster(this, 'EcsCluster', { vpc });

new ecsPatterns.ApplicationLoadBalancedFargateService(this, 'myService', {
  cluster,
  taskImageOptions: {
    image: ecs.ContainerImage.fromRegistry('amazon/amazon-ecs-sample'),
  },
});
```

`ApplicationLoadBalancedFargateService` creates a Fargate service behind an Application Load Balancer [1][2].

If you want to set the load balancer target group health check, `containerCpu`, `containerMemoryLimitMiB`, `targetProtocol`, `redirectHTTP`, or `recordType`, those are supported properties of the construct [1].

Use an ECR lifecycle policy with `tagStatus: "untagged"` and an expiration rule [3].

Based on [3]:
```json
{
  "rules": [
    {
      "rulePriority": 1,
      "description": "Keep only one untagged image, expire all others",
      "selection": {
        "tagStatus": "untagged",
        "countType": "imageCountMoreThan",
        "countNumber": 1
      },
      "action": {
        "type": "expire"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

Use a function named `handler` as the Lambda entry point, and have it take `event` and `context` arguments [3][4][5].

- Lambda runs the `handler` method when your function is invoked [1][3][4].
- The function code can be in `index.mjs`, and the handler export can look like `export const handler = async (event, context) => { ... }` [3][4].
- The handler configuration value is `index.handler` [3].
- AWS recommends using `async/await` for the handler instead of callbacks [2].

Based on [4]:

```js
export const handler = async (event, context) => {
  const length = event.length;
  const width = event.width;
  const area = length * width;
  return JSON.stringify({ area });
};
```

The `event` object contains the data your function processes, and the `context` object contains information about the invocation and execution environment [5].

Use CloudFront private content with an S3 origin and OAC, and allow only the CloudFront service principal in the bucket policy, scoped to your distribution with `AWS:SourceArn` [1][4].

- For a standard S3 origin, add an S3 bucket policy that allows `cloudfront.amazonaws.com` to use `s3:GetObject`, and include a `Condition` with the CloudFront distribution ARN in `AWS:SourceArn` [1].
- If you need write access through CloudFront, the example policy also allows `s3:PutObject` for the same CloudFront distribution [3].
- If your origin is an S3 Multi-Region Access Point, add a bucket policy to each underlying S3 bucket that allows the CloudFront service principal to access that bucket, and include the distribution ARN in `aws:SourceArn` [2][5].
- CloudFront says you can’t use OAC with an S3 bucket configured as a website endpoint; in that case, you must use the bucket as a custom origin instead [4].

Based on [1]:

```json
{
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": { "Service": "cloudfront.amazonaws.com" },
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::amzn-s3-demo-bucket/*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "AWS:SourceArn": "arn:aws:cloudfront::111122223333:distribution/{{CloudFront distribution ID}}"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

You can add a managed node group to an EKS cluster with the AWS Management Console, `eksctl`, or the CDK. [1][5][6]

- Before creating one, you need an existing Amazon EKS cluster and an existing IAM role for the nodes to use. [1]
- If you’re using the AWS Management Console, wait for the cluster status to be `ACTIVE`, open the Amazon EKS console, choose the cluster, go to **Compute** > **Node groups**, and choose **Add node group**. [2]
- If you’re using `eksctl`, managed node groups are defined with the `managedNodeGroups` field in the `ClusterConfig` file. [5]
- In the CDK, you can use `cluster.addNodegroupCapacity`, and if you want to apply customizations after instantiation, set the default capacity to 0 first. [6]

Based on [6] and [V]:
```ts
new eks.Cluster(this, 'HelloEKS', {
  version: eks.KubernetesVersion.V1_37,
  defaultCapacity: 0,
});

cluster.addNodegroupCapacity('ng', {
  desiredSize: 2,
});
```
